WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.468
A question enters an encoder.

00:00:02.644 --> 00:00:06.245
Out come tokens, vocabulary identifiers,

00:00:06.265 --> 00:00:07.856
initial vectors, contextual

00:00:07.876 --> 00:00:10.549
representations, and eventually one vector

00:00:10.569 --> 00:00:11.927
for the complete text.

00:00:12.115 --> 00:00:13.551
Which of these does a conventional

00:00:13.571 --> 00:00:16.206
single-vector dense retriever store and

00:00:16.226 --> 00:00:19.118
compare? Take a moment before following

00:00:19.138 --> 00:00:20.381
the assembly line.

00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:25.355
Only the final pooled representation plays

00:00:25.375 --> 00:00:26.398
that role here.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.834
The earlier units are working material

00:00:28.854 --> 00:00:30.547
inside the encoder.

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.842
Calling several of them tokens or

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:34.762
embeddings can hide the boundaries that

00:00:34.782 --> 00:00:36.179
matter for search.

00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:39.287
A lexical index term comes from text

00:00:39.307 --> 00:00:41.774
analysis and can lead directly to a

00:00:41.794 --> 00:00:44.789
posting list. A model token is an input

00:00:44.809 --> 00:00:47.173
unit processed with surrounding units

00:00:47.193 --> 00:00:48.887
inside an encoder.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.349
It does not acquire a posting list simply

00:00:51.369 --> 00:00:52.843
because we call it a token.

00:00:53.408 --> 00:00:55.451
The collection determines which analysed

00:00:55.471 --> 00:00:57.113
terms have postings.

00:00:57.262 --> 00:00:59.575
A pretrained model instead has a fixed

00:00:59.595 --> 00:01:02.834
input-token vocabulary or byte inventory.

00:01:03.022 --> 00:01:05.747
These processes serve different jobs;

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.899
their units are not interchangeable.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.374
Classic word two vec uses a fixed word

00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:14.634
vocabulary, so an unseen complete word may

00:01:14.654 --> 00:01:16.567
become an unknown token.

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:19.467
Modern encoders usually split unfamiliar

00:01:19.487 --> 00:01:23.062
strings into reusable subwords, characters

00:01:23.082 --> 00:01:26.541
or bytes. This improves input coverage.

00:01:27.118 --> 00:01:29.958
For our declared toy tokenizer, delulu

00:01:29.978 --> 00:01:35.241
becomes six characters: d, e, l, u, l, u.

00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:38.192
That is not a named model's tokenizer.

00:01:38.328 --> 00:01:40.692
Being able to split the slang does not

00:01:40.712 --> 00:01:42.548
establish that an encoder has learnt its

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:44.506
use or the searcher's need.

00:01:45.413 --> 00:01:48.055
In our toy vocabulary, d has identifier

00:01:48.075 --> 00:01:51.277
three, e eight, l eleven, and u

00:01:51.297 --> 00:01:54.111
twenty-one. The sequence becomes three,

00:01:54.389 --> 00:01:57.385
eight, eleven, twenty-one, eleven,

00:01:57.405 --> 00:02:00.078
twenty-one. These identifiers are lookup

00:02:00.098 --> 00:02:03.609
keys, not similarity scores or the final

00:02:03.629 --> 00:02:04.974
embedding dimensions.

00:02:05.514 --> 00:02:08.050
A lookup supplies an initial vector for

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.493
each identifier.

00:02:09.668 --> 00:02:12.199
Both occurrences of l start from the same

00:02:12.219 --> 00:02:13.835
toy lookup vector.

00:02:14.011 --> 00:02:16.001
Their representations can later differ

00:02:16.021 --> 00:02:18.553
because they occupy different places in

00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:20.640
the sequence. All of these numbers are

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:23.243
teaching values, not measured encoder

00:02:23.263 --> 00:02:24.235
outputs.

00:02:25.137 --> 00:02:27.226
The contextual encoder combines

00:02:27.246 --> 00:02:29.571
information across the sequence.

00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:31.801
It still produces per-token

00:02:31.821 --> 00:02:33.128
representations.

00:02:33.303 --> 00:02:35.113
Pooling then combines that working

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.574
material into one representation for the

00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:39.880
complete query or passage.

00:02:40.043 --> 00:02:42.291
Averaging is one option; a

00:02:42.311 --> 00:02:44.391
sequence-opening representation is

00:02:44.411 --> 00:02:45.151
another.

00:02:45.691 --> 00:02:47.952
Our six supplied three-number vectors sum

00:02:47.972 --> 00:02:50.593
to six, six, twelve.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:53.352
Divide each total by six and the mean is

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:57.153
one, one, two. This arithmetic illustrates

00:02:57.173 --> 00:02:59.705
pooling, not an actual language-model

00:02:59.725 --> 00:03:02.334
computation. Pooling alone does not

00:03:02.354 --> 00:03:04.317
guarantee useful retrieval.

00:03:05.233 --> 00:03:07.788
In a bi-encoder arrangement, a query and a

00:03:07.808 --> 00:03:10.443
passage are encoded independently, then

00:03:10.463 --> 00:03:12.569
their output vectors are compared.

00:03:12.744 --> 00:03:15.456
Their encoding paths can share parameters

00:03:15.476 --> 00:03:17.040
or use different parameters.

00:03:17.203 --> 00:03:19.464
Matching the arrangement and training to

00:03:19.484 --> 00:03:20.790
the task matters.

00:03:21.347 --> 00:03:24.048
Independent passage encoding means its

00:03:24.068 --> 00:03:26.445
vector can be prepared before a new query

00:03:26.465 --> 00:03:29.190
arrives. The passage need not be encoded

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.097
again with every question.

00:03:31.272 --> 00:03:33.546
Chapter Seven takes this arrangement to a

00:03:33.566 --> 00:03:36.239
whole collection; here we are identifying

00:03:36.259 --> 00:03:37.837
what produces the comparable

00:03:37.857 --> 00:03:39.202
representations.

00:03:40.108 --> 00:03:43.257
Return to the assembly line: text, model

00:03:43.277 --> 00:03:46.311
tokens, identifiers, initial vectors,

00:03:46.331 --> 00:03:49.172
contextual representations, pooling, one

00:03:49.192 --> 00:03:52.316
embedding. Model tokens are inputs; they

00:03:52.336 --> 00:03:54.700
are not the dimensions of the final vector

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.710
and are not automatically searchable

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.921
lexical terms.

00:03:58.457 --> 00:04:00.536
This chapter follows a conventional

00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:02.159
single-vector arrangement.

00:04:02.334 --> 00:04:04.904
Late-interaction methods retain multiple

00:04:04.924 --> 00:04:07.687
token vectors for comparison instead.

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.407
Producing a stored representation is one

00:04:10.427 --> 00:04:12.623
decision. Teaching its comparisons to

00:04:12.643 --> 00:04:15.458
reward useful passages is the next one.
